digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "Implicit_Alkane_Need" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Given few methods exist for radiosynthesis and there is a clear need for methodologies, therefore there is a specific gap for 18F-CF2H-alkanes."];
  "Implicit_CF2H_Interest" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Given interest has surged and driven by potential advantages, therefore CF2H is recognized as valuable."];
  "Implicit_CF2H_Props" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given CF2H acts as H-bond donor and serves as bioisosteres, therefore CF2H improves pharmacokinetic properties."];
  "Implicit_CF2H_Value" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Given CF2H is recognized as valuable and improves pharmacokinetic properties, therefore CF2H is valuable for biomolecule design."];
  "Implicit_Challenge" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Given challenge is to meet need for high molar activity and methods give low molar activity due to C-F cleavage, therefore C-F stability is the key barrier."];
  "Implicit_Demand" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Given F-18 radiotracer development is critical and CF2H is valuable for biomolecule design, therefore demand for 18F-CF2H grows."];
  "Implicit_F18_Access" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given F-18 is commonly used and accessible, therefore F-18 availability is high."];
  "Implicit_F18_Phys" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given F-18 has pure decay and long half-life, therefore F-18 physical properties are favorable."];
  "Implicit_Features" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given method enables efficient syntheses and method is amenable for automation, therefore the method has attractive features."];
  "Implicit_Gap" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Given existing approaches fail and there is a specific gap for 18F-CF2H-alkanes, therefore there is a significant gap in 18F-CF2H-alkane synthesis."];
  "Implicit_Gap_1" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Given current methods are insufficient and non-radioactive methods cannot solve the 18F problem, therefore existing approaches fail."];
  "Implicit_Method_Fail" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Given standard methods have limitations for 18F-CF2H and molar activity is a consistent issue, therefore current methods are insufficient for 18F-CF2H-alkanes."];
  "Implicit_Molar_Issue" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given methods give low molar activity due to C-F cleavage and arene methods provide mediocre molar activities, therefore molar activity is a consistent issue."];
  "Implicit_Need" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Given C-F stability is the key barrier and there is a significant gap in 18F-CF2H-alkane synthesis, therefore there is a clear need for simple and efficient methodologies at high molar activity."];
  "Implicit_NonRadio_Limit" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Given non-radioactive methods were achieved and are not adaptable to F-18, therefore they cannot solve the 18F problem."];
  "Implicit_PET_Context" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Given PET is widely applied and utility derives from radiotracers, therefore radiotracer development is critical."];
  "Implicit_Problem_Def" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Given demand for 18F-CF2H grows and there is a clear need for simple and efficient methodologies, therefore the problem is defined as high molar activity 18F-CF2H synthesis."];
  "Implicit_Std_Limit" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-reasoning: Given standard radiochemistry is well-established and success has not translated to multiple fluorine atoms, therefore standard methods have limitations for 18F-CF2H."];
  "Implicit_Std_Methods" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Given methods are based on nucleophilic substitution and generate tracers with high molar activities, therefore standard radiochemistry is well-established."];
  "Implicit_Vision" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Given we envisioned alpha-fluoroalkane with leaving group and the problem is defined as high molar activity 18F-CF2H synthesis, therefore this vision provides a potential solution."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study proposes a simple, automatable route for late-stage PET tracer synthesis in which direct nucleophilic substitution of α-bromo-α-fluoroalkane precursors with no-carrier-added [18F]fluoride furnishes 18F-labeled α,α-difluoromethylalkanes in good yields and at high molar activity.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) The retained and repaired terminal conclusions jointly motivate the paper-level research proposal."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) Positron emission tomography (PET) is an advanced imaging modality widely applied in biomedical research."];
  "S12_V1" [label="(12,1,0) The success achieved in constructing C(sp2)–18F bonds has not been readily translated to 18F-labeled compounds containing multiple fluorine atoms."];
  "S13_V1" [label="(13,1,0) Interest in the development of methods for incorporating the alpha,alpha-difluoromethyl group into modern pharmaceuticals has surged."];
  "S15_V1" [label="(15,1,0) The interest is driven by a recognition of the potential of the difluoromethyl group to confer distinct advantages in biomolecule designs."];
  "S2_V1" [label="(2,1,0) The broad utility of PET derives from well-designed radiotracers."];
  "S30_V1" [label="(30,1,0) Methods for attaching non-radioactive difluoromethyl groups to aliphatic chains were achieved after 2020 with various agents."];
  "S31_V1" [label="(31,1,0) These methods are not adaptable to fluorine-18 labeling due to harsh reaction conditions and likely outcomes of high carrier dilution."];
  "S32_V1" [label="(32,1,0) To date, few methods exist for the radiosynthesis of an 18F-labeled alpha,alpha-difluoromethylalkane."];
  "S38_V1" [label="(38,1,0) There is a clear need for the development of simple and efficient broad-scope methodologies for labeling alpha,alpha-difluoromethylalkanes with fluorine-18 at high molar activity."];
  "S39_V1" [label="(39,1,0) We envisioned that [18F]fluoride treatment of an alpha-fluoroalkane possessing a leaving group on the alpha-carbon might provide an 18F-labeled alpha,alpha-difluoromethylalkane with high molar activity."];
  "S3_V1" [label="(3,1,0) Fluorine-18 (18F) is one of the most commonly used positron-emitting radionuclides for PET."];
  "S40_V2" [label="(40,2,0) The authors' method enables the efficient late-stage syntheses of 18F-labeled alpha,alpha-difluoromethylalkanes with high molar activities."];
  "S41_V1" [label="(41,1,0) The method has several other attractive features, including amenability for automation."];
  "S4_V1" [label="(4,1,0) Fluorine-18 is highly accessible in a no-carrier-added (NCA) state from modern cyclotrons."];
  "S5_V1" [label="(5,1,0) Fluorine-18 has almost pure decay by emission of a relatively low energy positron enabling high spatial resolution."];
  "S6_V1" [label="(6,1,0) Fluorine-18 has a usefully long half-life that allows time for efficient incorporation into tracers."];
